            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-804eb7da59a4ae3341c2\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"Arles is a city in southern France, on the banks of the Rhône in Provence-Alpes-Côte d’Azur. Its identity is shaped by a long urban history: Roman monuments—including an amphitheatre, theatre and underground galleries—stand alongside medieval Romanesque buildings such as Saint-Trophime and its cloister. This surviving mix is significant because it shows how an ancient city was adapted through the medieval period; UNESCO inscribed Arles’s Roman and Romanesque monuments on the World Heritage List in 1981. Arles is also closely associated with Vincent van Gogh, who lived there from February 1888 to May 1889 and painted local scenes, including the Rhône waterfront. Its river setting, layered heritage and artistic connections distinguish the city.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/164\",\"https://www.arlestourisme.com/index.php?id=16\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"Arles lies on the Rhône in the Provence-Alpes-Côte d’Azur region of southern France.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/164\"]},{\"text\":\"The city preserves Roman sites such as its amphitheatre, theatre and underground galleries, as well as the Romanesque church and cloister of Saint-Trophime.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/164\"]},{\"text\":\"According to the city’s heritage history, Roman veterans founded a colony at Arles in 46 BCE.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://patrimoine.ville-arles.fr/\"]},{\"text\":\"UNESCO inscribed Arles’s Roman and Romanesque monuments on the World Heritage List in 1981, recognizing the city as an example of an ancient city adapted to medieval civilization.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://whc.unesco.org/en/list/164\"]},{\"text\":\"Vincent van Gogh lived in Arles from February 1888 to May 1889 and painted local places, including the Rhône waterfront.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.arlestourisme.com/index.php?id=16\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"